<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="58646" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHYS/598/58646.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="PHYS" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHYS.xml">Physics</subject><course id="598" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHYS/598.xml">Special Topics in Physics</course></parents><sectionNumber>JYL</sectionNumber><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Many-body Physics of Information As quantum information science becomes increasingly central to cutting-edge research in quantum physics, understanding its relationship with many-body physics is crucial. Despite the deep interconnections between these fields, few courses present them in a unified and coherent manner. This course addresses that gap, offering an in-depth exploration of the intersection between quantum information science and quantum many-body physics.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LEC">Lecture</type><start>09:30 AM</start><end>10:50 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>136</roomNumber><buildingName>Loomis Laboratory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Lee" firstName="J">Lee, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>